&ldquo;This. I scarcely slept last night, for thinking of the dangers you are
      running. The Paladin told me how you made the duke stand out of the way
      when the cannon-balls were flying all about, and so saved his life.&rdquo;

&ldquo;Oh, I believe it, I believe it, and I am so glad! Then you will come back
      and bide with us all your life long, and we will love you so, and honor
      you!&rdquo;

&ldquo;It will be the most noble and beneficent victory that God has vouchsafed
      for France at any time. I pray you question me not as to whence or how I
      know this thing, but be content that it is so.&rdquo;

&ldquo;Yes. And I will be with you at Meung with the dawn, fetching the
      Constable and his fifteen hundred; and when Talbot knows that Beaugency
      has fallen it will have an effect upon him.&rdquo;

&ldquo;Name of God, what would you? We must smite these English, and we will.
      They shall not escape us. Though they were hung to the clouds we would get
      them!&rdquo;

&ldquo;Nenni, en nom de Dieu! These English are ours&mdash;they are lost. They
      will fly. Who overtakes them will need good spurs. Forward&mdash;close
      up!&rdquo;

&ldquo;The Maid of Orleans undertook this expedition of her own motion; and it
      is my mind that it is her judgment that should be followed here, and not
      that of any other, let him be of whatsoever breed and standing he may.&rdquo;

&ldquo;You have saved the crown. Speak&mdash;require&mdash;demand; and
      whatsoever grace you ask it shall be granted, though it make the kingdom
      poor to meet it.&rdquo;

&ldquo;Then, O gentle King, if out of your compassion you will speak the word, I
      pray you give commandment that my village, poor and hard pressed by reason
      of war, may have its taxes remitted.&rdquo;

__________________________________ | | | DOMREMI | | | | RIEN&mdash;LA
      PUCELLE | |__________________________________| &ldquo;NOTHING&mdash;THE MAID OF
      ORLEANS.&rdquo;

&ldquo;Don&rsquo;t, father! it breaks my heart. I will be so good to her when I get
      home. I will do her work for her, and be her comfort, and she shall not
      suffer any more through me.&rdquo;

&ldquo;You have done the will of God, dear, and are quits; it is true, and none
      may deny it; but what of the King? You are his best soldier; what if he
      command you to stay?&rdquo;

&ldquo;The King is my Lord; I am his servant.&rdquo; She was silent and thoughtful a
      little while, then she brightened up and said, cheerily, &ldquo;But let us drive
      such thoughts away&mdash;this is no time for them. Tell me about home.&rdquo;

And old D&rsquo;Arc stood looking at her the same way, sort of absently
      scratching his head; but had to give it up, and said he didn&rsquo;t know&mdash;&ldquo;must
      have been something that happened when we weren&rsquo;t noticing.&rdquo;

&ldquo;Every sane man&mdash;whose loyalty is to his King and not a show and a
      pretense&mdash;knows that there is but one rational thing before us&mdash;the
      march upon Paris!&rdquo;

&ldquo;Peace. She had a right to be consulted before that thing was undertaken,
      since it concerned war as well as politics. It is but just that she be
      heard upon it now.&rdquo;

&ldquo;I cry you mercy!&rdquo; interrupted the Chancellor, who saw a dangerous
      enthusiasm rising in the King&rsquo;s face. &ldquo;March upon Paris? Does your
      Excellency forget that the way bristles with English strongholds?&rdquo;

&ldquo;Stay!&rdquo; cried the Chancellor. &ldquo;It would be madness to put our affront upon
      his Highness the Duke of Burgundy. By the treaty which we have every hope
      to make with him&mdash;&rdquo;

&ldquo;Sire, it is madness, sheer madness! Your Excellency, we cannot, we must
      not go back from what we have done; we have proposed to treat, we must
      treat with the Duke of Burgundy.&rdquo;

&ldquo;Ah, if it might but please God to let me put off this steel raiment and
      go back to my father and my mother, and tend my sheep again with my sister
      and my brothers, who would be so glad to see me!&rdquo;

&ldquo;It is true, I have wanted to escape, and I do want to escape.&rdquo; Then she
      added, in a way that would touch the pity of anybody, I think, &ldquo;It is the
      right of every prisoner.&rdquo;

&ldquo;Now, Joan, the matter is very simple; just speak up and frankly and truly
      answer the questions which I am going to ask you, as you have sworn to
      do.&rdquo;

&ldquo;As a rule, witnesses are but dull creatures, and an easy prey&mdash;yes,
      and easily embarrassed, easily frightened&mdash;but truly one can neither
      scare this child nor find her dozing.&rdquo;

&ldquo;I was thirteen when I first heard a Voice coming from God to help me to
      live well. I was frightened. It came at midday, in my father&rsquo;s garden in
      the summer.&rdquo;

&ldquo;No, I was to go to Vaucouleurs, and Robert de Baudricourt would give me
      soldiers to go with me to France; and I answered, saying that I was a poor
      girl who did not know how to ride, neither how to fight.&rdquo;

&ldquo;It required me to remain behind at St. Denis. I would have obeyed if I
      had been free, but I was helpless by my wound, and the knights carried me
      away by force.&rdquo;

&ldquo;Yes, I thanked it; and knelt in my bed where I was chained, and joined my
      hands and begged it to implore God&rsquo;s help for me so that I might have
      light and instruction as touching the answers I should give here.&rdquo;

&ldquo;I believe wholly&mdash;as wholly as I believe the Christian faith and
      that God has redeemed us from the fires of hell, that God speaks to me by
      that Voice!&rdquo;

&ldquo;The Voice has commanded me to tell the King certain things, and not you&mdash;and
      some very lately&mdash;even last night; things which I would he knew. He
      would be more easy at his dinner.&rdquo;

&ldquo;Yes, and by command of God. But that it was His will I would not have
      come. I would sooner have had my body torn in sunder by horses than come,
      lacking that.&rdquo;

Presently she added: &ldquo;During three weeks I was questioned by the clergy at
      Chinon and Poitiers. The King had a sign before he would believe; and the
      clergy were of opinion that my acts were good and not evil.&rdquo;

&ldquo;The sword of the Burgundian Franquet d&rsquo;Arras, whom I took prisoner in the
      engagement at Lagny. I kept it because it was a good war-sword&mdash;good
      to lay on stout thumps and blows with.&rdquo;

&ldquo;In the final assault at Orleans did you tell your soldiers that the
      arrows shot by the enemy and the stones discharged from their catapults
      would not strike any one but you?&rdquo;

&ldquo;I had two rings. They have been taken away from me during my captivity.
      You have one of them. It is the gift of my brother. Give it back to me. If
      not to me, then I pray that it be given to the Church.&rdquo;

&ldquo;I have always said that I was not permitted to tell you everything. I am
      to be set free, and I desire to ask leave of my Voices to tell you the
      day. That is why I wish for delay.&rdquo;

The soldier-spirit in Joan was offended at this puerility. She drew
      herself up, and said with dignity and fire: &ldquo;What I said to them was,
      &lsquo;Ride those English down!&rsquo; and I did it myself.&rdquo;

&ldquo;While we prayed it came to life, and cried. It had been dead three days,
      and was as black as my doublet. It was straight way baptized, then it
      passed from life again and was buried in holy ground.&rdquo;

&ldquo;No. It was but an act of devotion. And it is the custom of men of war who
      have been wounded to make such offering there. I had been wounded before
      Paris.&rdquo;

&ldquo;Yes&mdash;for I should see in that the permission of Our Lord. God helps
      who help themselves, the proverb says. But except I thought I had
      permission, I would not go.&rdquo;

&ldquo;As the Voices have told you you are going to Paradise, you feel certain
      that that will happen and that you will not be damned in hell. Is that
      so?&rdquo;

&ldquo;I am come to the King of France from the Church Triumphant on high by its
      commandant, and to that Church I will submit all those things which I have
      done. For the Church Militant I have no other answer now.&rdquo;

&ldquo;As to the Duke of Burgundy, I required of him, both by letters and by his
      ambassadors, that he make peace with the King. As to the English, the only
      peace for them was that they leave the country and go home.&rdquo;

&ldquo;If they had listened to me,&rdquo; said she, &ldquo;they would have done wisely.&rdquo; At
      this point she uttered her prophecy again, saying with emphasis, &ldquo;Before
      seven years they will see it themselves.&rdquo;

&ldquo;I seem to be in danger of death from this malady; if it be the pleasure
      of God that I die here, I beg that I may be heard in confession and also
      receive my Saviour; and that I may be buried in consecrated ground.&rdquo;

&ldquo;Verily it is a wonderful creature. She has laid her hand upon an accepted
      truth that is as old as the world, and it has crumbled to dust and rubbish
      under her touch. Now whence got she that marvelous insight?&rdquo;

&ldquo;By my faith, sir! I make bold to say and swear, on pain of death, that he
      is the most noble Christian of all Christians, and the best lover of the
      faith and the Church!&rdquo;

&ldquo;As to that matter, I have answered my judges before. I have told them to
      report all that I have said and done to our Holy Father the Pope&mdash;to
      whom, and to God first, I appeal.&rdquo;

&ldquo;Sign! sign!&rdquo; from the priests; &ldquo;sign&mdash;sign and be saved!&rdquo; And
      Loyseleur was urging at her ear, &ldquo;Do as I told you&mdash;do not destroy
      yourself!&rdquo;

&ldquo;And that she may repent of her crimes and repeat them no more, she is
      sentenced to perpetual imprisonment, with the bread of affliction and the
      water of anguish!&rdquo;

&ldquo;Alas,&rdquo; she said, &ldquo;if you had put me in the Church&rsquo;s prison, and given me
      right and proper keepers, as you promised, this would not have happened.
      And for this I summon you to answer before God!&rdquo;
